  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Which states require specific certificate of trust content?</summary><p>Most do. States that have adopted the Uniform Trust Code accept a certificate containing the § 1013 elements, and the remaining states have their own statutory content lists — every state page in this directory cites its statute. Delaware, Texas, and Ohio are common examples pages people check first, each with its own citation and execution rules.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>What is the difference between a certificate of trust and a declaration of trust?</summary><p>The declaration creates the trust and states its terms; the certificate is a short summary of an existing trust that banks and title companies accept instead of the full agreement. Many people who create a declaration here also generate a certificate for banking.</p></details>
      <details class="faq-item"><summary>Does the certificate need to be notarized?</summary><p>State statutes usually leave notarization to the receiving institution. Some states, like California, require an acknowledged (notarized) certification by statute — the state pages note where that applies.</p></details>
    </div>
